<newsitem itemid="111411" id="root" date="1996-10-11" xml:lang="en">
<title>USA: U.S. Sept producer prices rose 0.2 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Sept producer prices rose 0.2 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				Sept    Aug   Sept96/95</p>
<p> Finished Goods		0.2    0.3     2.9</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.3   -0.1     1.4</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	     0.2    1.0     4.2</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		 0.2    0.7     7.1</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  131.6  131.9     N/A</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     Sept    Aug   Sept96/95</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     0.9    0.9    14.2</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		  2.0    4.1    25.3</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	  -1.0   -0.2     2.1</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	     1.2   -0.2     2.5</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	  0.3   -0.1     1.1</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  0.3    0.2     0.5</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1    0.1    -1.6</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	 0.2   Unch    -2.0</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.4    0.3     1.1</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	 Sept    Aug   Sept96/95</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods    0.8    0.9     8.9</p>
<p> Crude Goods		  -1.5    0.2    10.2</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	0.6    0.1   -10.3</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -3.8   -0.3    14.6</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	  0.6    0.4     7.0</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	 0.6    0.7    20.9</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    7.3    3.2    28.6</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast September producer prices to rise 0.3 percent. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.2 percent increase.  </p>
<p>The department said the increase in passenger car prices in September was the largest since a 1.3 percent jump in November 1993. It also said tobacco in September posted its largest drop since a 25.7 percent decline in August 1993.</p>
<p>The rise in the PPI excluding food and energy was the biggest since a 0.3 percent increase in May 1996.</p>
</text>
